IFL_DATA.register("comparisons", {"aspects":[{"key":"category","label":"Category","origin":"f1"},{"key":"nature","label":"Nature of contract","origin":"f1"},{"key":"ownership","label":"Ownership and asset risk","origin":"f1"},{"key":"return","label":"Return to financier","origin":"f1"},{"key":"payment","label":"Payment / delivery timing","origin":"f1"},{"key":"late","label":"Late payment (Box 14.1)","origin":"f1"},{"key":"tenor","label":"Typical tenor (Box 14.1)","origin":"f1"},{"key":"uses","label":"Typical uses","origin":"f1"},{"key":"keyRule","label":"Key Shari’ah rule","origin":"f1"},{"key":"pitfall","label":"Common pitfall","origin":"f1"},{"key":"shariahBasis","label":"Shari’ah basis","origin":"f2"},{"key":"subjectMatter","label":"Subject matter","origin":"f2"},{"key":"ownershipRiskTiming","label":"Ownership, risk and timing","origin":"f2"},{"key":"returnType","label":"Return type","origin":"f2"},{"key":"returnTypeDetail","label":"Return detail","origin":"f2"},{"key":"typicalTenor","label":"Typical tenor (detail)","origin":"f2"},{"key":"liquidityTradability","label":"Liquidity and tradability","origin":"f2"},{"key":"commonUse","label":"Common use (detail)","origin":"f2"},{"key":"keyConditions","label":"Key conditions","origin":"f2","list":true},{"key":"majorRisks","label":"Major risks","origin":"f2","list":true},{"key":"distinguishingFeature","label":"Distinguishing feature","origin":"f2"}],"pairs":[{"id":"riba-trade","title":"Riba vs trade (sale, loan and lease distinguished)","topicId":"t3.2.1.b","columns":["Trade (Bai‘)","Loan (Qard) with Riba"],"rows":[["What passes","Ownership of goods, permanently","Money, to be returned in the same kind"],["Return","Profit margin in the agreed price","Any stipulated excess is Riba"],["Risk","Seller bears goods/market risk until sale","Lender's principal is guaranteed"],["Credit period","Higher credit price agreed before sale; fixed thereafter","Increase grows with time"],["Qur’anic ruling","‘Allah has permitted trade’ (2:275)","‘…and forbidden Riba’ (2:275)"],["Nature of the return","A guaranteed increment on a loan of money over time, unrelated to any exchange of real goods or services.","A margin earned as part of a genuine exchange of goods for value between a seller and buyer."],["Risk allocation","The lender's return is guaranteed regardless of outcome; the borrower alone bears the risk.","Follows the principle 'Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman' — a party may claim profit only if it is also ready to bear liability/business risk (e.g. damage in storage or transit, market and price risk)."],["Basis of legitimacy","No service is rendered and no risk is borne; money simply earns more money.","Justified because the trader provides a definite service (seeking out, locating and purchasing goods) and bears real business risk."],["Economic effect","Creates a 'rentier class' that takes a growing share of national output without contributing to the creation of real wealth.","Circulates real goods and contributes directly to the development of resources and society."],["Qur'anic sequencing","Prohibited in the same verse pair (2:275-276) that permits trading.","The permissibility of trading is mentioned before the prohibition of Riba, signifying that trade is the Shari'ah's intended alternative to Riba."],["Price/payment timing","The amount owed can effectively keep growing with time (the essence of interest).","A trader may charge a higher credit price than the cash price, but the price, once agreed at the bargain, cannot be changed thereafter even if payment is delayed."]],"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["riba-vs-trade"],"titleAlt":"Riba (Interest) vs Trade Profit","summary":"The book infers from Qur'an 2:275-276 that Riba on loans/debts must never be equated with trade profit, even though both are a 'return' on capital — the Shari'ah basis for treating them so differently is risk, service and ownership.","source":{"chapters":[3,6],"sections":["3.2.1","6.3.1"],"pages":[44,132]},"columnsAlt":["Riba (Interest)","Trade Profit (Bai')"]},{"id":"trade-profit-interest","title":"Trade profit vs interest (Section 6.3.1)","topicId":"t6.3.1","columns":["Trade profit","Interest"],"rows":[["Source","Real activity: finding, buying, storing and selling goods","Time on money lent"],["Risk","Storage, transit, market and price risks borne","Reward guaranteed to lender"],["After contract","Price fixed once agreed","Accrues further on delay"]],"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"murabaha-musawamah","title":"Murabaha vs Musawamah","topicId":"t9.11","columns":["Murabaha","Musawamah"],"rows":[["Cost disclosure","Cost and margin disclosed","Price bargained; cost not disclosed"],["Suitability","Where cost is precisely known","Where cost can't be ascertained or supplier discounts are not passed on"],["Other conditions","Ownership, possession and risk rules","Same ownership, possession and risk rules"],["Cost disclosure (extended)","A fiduciary sale (Bai' al Amanah) — the seller must honestly disclose the original cost and any add-on expenses.","The seller is under no obligation to reveal cost; price is simply bargained between the parties."],["Basis of price","Cost plus a stipulated, mutually agreed profit margin.","A price freely negotiated with no reference at all to the seller's cost."],["Nature of trust required","Described as a 'contract of trustworthiness' — the buyer relies on the seller's honest cost statement.","No such trust element is needed, since no cost figure is being relied upon."],["Typical suitability","Where the buyer wants transparency of cost and markup, or lacks the skill to bargain well himself.","Where the seller cannot precisely ascertain his own cost, for very large single transactions where only the final price matters, or where a bank has obtained an undisclosed supplier discount."],["Benchmarking of profit","Determining profit against a benchmark is comparatively easier, since the cost base is disclosed.","Harder to benchmark, since the cost base is never disclosed to the buyer."],["Applicable conditions","Must meet the general conditions of a valid Bai' plus Murabaha's own specific conditions (real, existing, owned, lawful subject matter; no currency or debt as subject matter).","The same general conditions of a valid Bai' and Murabaha's specific conditions apply equally to Musawamah."]],"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["murabaha-vs-musawamah"],"titleAlt":"Murabaha vs Musawamah","summary":"Both are ordinary sales (Bai') on either a cash or deferred basis and both must satisfy the same general conditions of a valid sale; they differ only in whether the seller's original cost is disclosed as the basis for the price.","source":{"chapters":[9],"sections":["9.2","9.3","9.6","9.11"],"pages":[214,215,217,234]},"columnsAlt":["Murabaha","Musawamah"]},{"id":"salam-murabaha","title":"Salam vs Murabaha (Box 10.2)","topicId":"t10.10","columns":["Salam","Murabaha"],"rows":[["Delivery","Deferred","Spot"],["Price","Paid in full at contract","Spot or deferred"],["Subject","Goods by specification","Particular existing commodities"],["Excluded goods","Things requiring spot exchange (e.g. wheat for barley)","Can be executed in them"]],"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"ijarah-bai","title":"Ijarah vs Bai‘ (Section 11.2.1)","topicId":"t11.2.1","columns":["Bai‘ (sale)","Ijarah (lease)"],"rows":[["What passes","Ownership of the corpus","Usufruct only"],["Duration","Permanent","Time-bound"],["Ownership risk and expenses","Buyer (after sale)","Lessor"],["Consideration","Price","Rent"],["If transferee becomes owner","—","Ijarah ceases"],["What is transferred","Ownership of the corpus of the property itself is transferred to the purchaser.","Only the usufruct (the right to use the asset) is transferred to the lessee; ownership of the corpus stays with the lessor."],["Duration (extended)","A definite, instant transfer of ownership; there is no comparable time limit built into the contract.","Always time-bound — the lease must terminate at some point in time."],["Effect of the lessee acquiring the corpus","Not applicable — ownership is the intended and immediate outcome of the sale itself.","If the lessee becomes owner of the corpus by any other means (e.g. gift or inheritance), the Ijarah automatically ceases to be in force."],["Ownership-related risk","Passes to the buyer along with title once the sale is executed.","Remains with the lessor (owner) throughout the lease term."],["Certainty requirement","The price and the sold item must similarly be certain and free of Gharar.","The usufruct and its counter-value (rent) must be known comprehensively to avoid Gharar."]],"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ijarah-vs-bai"],"titleAlt":"Ijarah (Lease) vs Bai' (Sale)","summary":"Ijarah resembles a sale in that something valuable is transferred for a consideration, but the book is explicit (11.2.1) that what is transferred, and for how long, differ fundamentally.","source":{"chapters":[11],"sections":["11.2.1"],"pages":[280]},"columnsAlt":["Ijarah","Bai'"]},{"id":"musharakah-debt","title":"Musharakah vs debt financing (Sections 4.3, 12.3)","topicId":"t4.3","columns":["Musharakah (equity)","Interest-based debt"],"rows":[["Financier's return","Agreed share of actual profit","Pre-fixed interest"],["Loss","Shared by capital ratio","Borne by borrower; principal guaranteed"],["Guarantee of capital","Not allowed (only against negligence)","Principal and interest secured"],["Effect on economy","Supplies risk capital, encourages entrepreneurship","Debt grows with time"],["Basis of the return","A variable profit share tied to the actual performance of the joint venture, agreed only as a ratio, never a fixed amount or a percentage of capital.","A price or rent fixed once and for all at the time of contract (Murabaha profit, Ijarah rental), or proceeds determined by the market at the time of sale (Salam)."],["Loss-bearing and guarantees","Loss is borne by the partners strictly in proportion to their capital; the capital or profit of any partner can never be guaranteed by a co-partner, only security against a partner's own negligence or misconduct is permissible.","Once validly created, the debt/receivable is a fixed, guaranteed obligation of the client regardless of how the client's own business performs."],["Suitability for risk-averse investors","Exposes the investor to genuine business risk, so it is not advisable for a depositor who cannot bear the risk of capital loss.","The book's own example: a widow who cannot bear Shirkah-type risk should have her funds invested by the bank in less risky, Shari'ah-compliant trade- and Ijarah-based activities instead."],["Standing among jurists (theory)","Considered by the majority of jurists on Islamic finance to be the most desirable mode, since it embodies genuine risk-sharing and entrepreneurship.","Accepted as legitimate — debt itself is not prohibited, only interest on it — but regarded by the pioneers of the discipline as a fallback rather than the ideal."],["Actual market share (practice)","The book notes that in practice the share of PLS (Musharakah/Mudarabah) business in Islamic banks is negligible.","The mainstream business of Islamic financial institutions is based on debt-creating modes earning fixed returns, a gap between theory and practice that critics highlight."],["Management involvement","All partners have a right to participate in managing the joint business, though they may forgo this in favour of one specific partner.","The financier's involvement is limited to the sale/lease transaction itself; once the price or rent is fixed, it takes no further part in managing the client's business."]],"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["musharakah-vs-debt-financing"],"titleAlt":"Musharakah (Equity/PLS) vs Debt-Creating Modes","summary":"Section 4.3 reframes the usual 'debt versus equity' question as one of balance: debt-creating modes remain legitimate tools, but the book (12.1, 17.4.3) treats genuine profit/loss-sharing as the more desirable ideal that practice has largely fallen short of.","source":{"chapters":[4,12,17],"sections":["4.3","12.1","12.3.5","17.4.3"],"pages":[85,307,318,434,448]},"columnsAlt":["Musharakah (Equity/PLS)","Debt-Creating Modes (Murabaha, Ijarah, Salam)"]},{"id":"musharakah-mudarabah","title":"Musharakah vs Mudarabah","topicId":"t12.5","columns":["Musharakah","Mudarabah"],"rows":[["Capital","All partners","Rabbul-mal only"],["Management","All partners may work","Mudarib manages; Rabbul-mal oversees"],["Loss","By capital ratio","Rabbul-mal (Mudarib only if negligent)"],["Liability","Normally unlimited","Limited to investment"],["Distribution","Periodic by valuation","After liquidation; on-account allowed"],["Assets","Jointly owned; appreciation shared","Owned by Rabbul-mal"],["Dissolution","Money valuation not necessary","Assets valued in money"],["Source of capital","Investment comes from all the partners.","Capital comes from the investor(s) (Rabbul-mal) only, not from the working partner (Mudarib)."],["Right to manage","All partners have a right to participate in the management of the business and can work for it.","The Rabbul-mal has no right to participate in management, though he may work for the venture with mutual consent and may monitor the Mudarib's conduct."],["Loss-sharing","All partners share loss strictly in proportion to their capital investment.","Any financial loss is suffered by the Rabbul-mal alone, unless the Mudarib was negligent or dishonest."],["Liability (extended)","Liability of the partners is normally unlimited.","The Rabbul-mal's liability is limited to his investment, unless he authorized the Mudarib to incur debts on his behalf."],["Timing of profit distribution","Profit can be distributed periodically (annually, quarterly, or monthly) by valuing the assets.","Final distribution can only take place after liquidation of the Mudarabah business, though on-account payments subject to later adjustment are allowed."],["Ownership of business assets","All assets become jointly owned by the partners in proportion to their investment, so each benefits from appreciation even before a sale.","Assets purchased belong solely to the Rabbul-mal; the Mudarib earns only by profitably selling them."],["Dissolution (extended)","This monetary-valuation step is not necessary in the same way.","Assets and profit, if any, can be distributed only after their value is assessed in monetary terms."]],"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["mudarabah-vs-musharakah"],"titleAlt":"Mudarabah vs Musharakah","summary":"Section 12.5 distinguishes these two participatory (Shirkah-based) modes on seven grounds — the most frequently tested distinction in the participatory-finance chapters.","source":{"chapters":[12],"sections":["12.5"],"pages":[327,328]},"columnsAlt":["Mudarabah","Musharakah"]},{"id":"salam-istisna","title":"Salam vs Istisna‘a (Box 10.10)","topicId":"t10.11.11","columns":["Salam","Istisna‘a"],"rows":[["Subject","Natural or manufactured goods","Always needs manufacturing"],["Payment","Full price in advance","Need not be fully in advance"],["Nature of goods","Mithli (fungible)","Mainly Qimi (also trademarked items)"],["Late delivery","Penalty to charity","Price reduction to buyer (Shart-e-Jazai)"],["Binding","Binding once executed","Nonbinding until work starts"],["Parallel contract","Parallel Salam","Parallel Istisna‘a"],["Subject matter","Can be natural products or manufactured goods, but must already be produceable/obtainable in the market.","Always a thing that needs to be manufactured; the subject does not yet exist in finished form."],["Price payment timing","The full price must be paid in advance, on the spot, at the time of contract.","The price does not need to be paid in full in advance — it can be immediate, deferred, or paid in instalments."],["Fungibility of the subject","Must consist of fungible (Mithli) goods, since it is a liability on the seller who, if unable to produce it himself, must be able to source an equivalent from the market.","Mainly used for Qimi (non-fungible) goods whose units differ in price/specification, though it can also be used for standardized, trademarked items."],["Binding nature","Conclusive and binding from execution; it cannot be altered or revoked except with the consent of both parties.","Nonbinding as long as the manufacturer has not started work — either party may cancel before that; once work starts, it becomes binding and can be changed only by mutual consent."],["Penalty for delay","A penalty for late delivery goes to charity, leaving the bank's own profit-and-loss account unaffected.","A price-reduction penalty for delay is treated as the purchaser's income (the Shart-e-Jazai principle accepted by jurists)."],["Financing suitability","Best suited to short-, and in some cases medium-, term financing, especially in agriculture and agro-based industry.","Suited to short- or long-term financing of manufacturing/construction, with the flexibility to control delay through parallel contracting and staged payment."]],"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["salam-vs-istisnaa"],"titleAlt":"Salam vs Istisna'a","summary":"Both are forward-sale contracts that let payment and delivery be separated in time, but the book (Box 10.10) draws several precise distinctions between them.","source":{"chapters":[10],"sections":["10.4.2","10.9","10.11.3","10.11.5"],"pages":[246,255,265,266]},"columnsAlt":["Salam","Istisna'a"]},{"id":"murabaha-loan","title":"Murabaha vs interest-based loan","topicId":"t9.9","columns":["Murabaha","Conventional loan"],"rows":[["Subject","Goods","Money"],["Ownership","Bank owns goods before sale","No ownership of goods"],["Risk","Bank bears asset risk until sale","Lender bears credit risk only"],["Price on delay","Fixed; penalty to charity","Interest keeps accruing"],["Rollover","Not allowed","Common"],["Documentation","Invoices, agency, offer/acceptance","Loan agreement"]],"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"ijarah-lease","title":"Islamic Ijarah vs conventional financial lease","topicId":"t11.4.4","columns":["Ijarah / IMBT","Conventional financial lease"],"rows":[["Rent start","From usable delivery","From payment to supplier"],["Ownership risk","Lessor","Lessee"],["Theft/destruction","Rent stops; lessor bears loss","Rent continues until insurance settles"],["Insurance","Takaful at lessor's cost","Insurance by lessee"],["Late payment","Charity undertaking","Late fee as income"],["Transfer","Separate sale/gift","Built into contract"],["Early termination","Only due rent","Remaining rent may be claimed"],["When rent starts","Rent may only be charged from the date the lessee is actually able to benefit from the asset, i.e. after delivery.","The lessor often starts charging rental as soon as funds are released to the supplier — effectively leasing an asset before buying it or taking possession."],["Who bears ownership risk/major repairs","The lessor must bear expenses to rectify defects that prevent the lessee from using the asset; the lessee is responsible only for day-to-day maintenance/running costs.","The lessor often shifts nearly all risk to the lessee, particularly where the residual value is fixed in advance."],["Operating lease risk/cost","The lessor must bear the upkeep responsibilities and all risks/costs of ownership even in an operating lease.","All risks and expenses are typically the lessee's responsibility."],["Structuring end-of-term ownership transfer","The lease agreement itself must not contain a precondition of sale or gift; instead the lessor may make a separate, independent unilateral promise to sell or gift the asset at the end of the term.","Hire-purchase/finance-lease agreements often mutually agree at the outset that the contract includes the sale of the asset, combining the two into one bargain."],["Why this avoids 'two contracts in one'","The Ijarah contract takes immediate effect; the promise to sell/gift may or may not become effective later and does not bind the lessee, so the two remain legally distinct.","Bundling the sale into the lease from the outset is what raises the Shari'ah objection of combining two different bargains in one."]],"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ijarah-vs-conventional-leasing"],"titleAlt":"Islamic Ijarah vs Conventional Leasing","summary":"Section 11.4.4 appraises modern conventional leases against Shari'ah principles and identifies several practices that a genuine Ijarah cannot replicate.","source":{"chapters":[11],"sections":["11.4.4","11.4.5"],"pages":[289,290]},"columnsAlt":["Islamic Ijarah","Conventional Lease"]},{"id":"tawarruq-inah","title":"Tawarruq vs Bai‘ al ‘Inah","topicId":"t13.3","columns":["Tawarruq","‘Inah"],"rows":[["Resale to","Third party","Original seller"],["Juristic view","Permissible (preferred view of four schools, AAOIFI)","Prohibited by majority"],["Economic effect","Cash obtained via real sale","Loan with interest via buy-back"],["Caution","Limit to unavoidable corporate liquidity","Avoid"],["Who the commodity is resold to","The Mutawarriq buys a commodity on credit and resells it to an unrelated third party for cash to obtain liquidity.","The buyer resells the commodity back to the very seller from whom it was originally purchased, at a different price."],["Underlying economic substance","A genuine purchase and sale of a real commodity in the market, even though the buyer's real objective is liquidity rather than the commodity itself.","A circular buy-back between the same two parties whose net result is a loan with interest, which is why the book treats it as a ruse (Hilah) used to circumvent Riba."],["Price relationship that triggers prohibition","The resale price to the third party is an independently determined, genuine spot-market price.","The classical objection (Ibn Qudama) targets exactly this: reselling to the same seller at a higher credit price than the cash price paid (or vice versa) within the same arrangement."],["Position across the schools of Fiqh","The preferred view in all four Sunni schools is that Tawarruq is permissible, though some later Hanafi jurists held it to be the same as 'Inah and hence abominable.","Jurists in general have prohibited 'Inah; even the Shafi'i school, sometimes cited as tolerant of it, is found by the book's cited research to lack any significant Shari'ah justification for it."],["AAOIFI's operative test","Acceptable where the commodity is sold to a third party, even indirectly through an agent.","Invalid where the commodity is sold back, directly or indirectly, to the original seller from whom it was purchased on a deferred-payment basis."],["Modern institutional practice","Used by many Islamic banks for liquidity management and personal/credit-card financing, typically via organized or commodity Tawarruq, though the book cautions this needs care to remain a genuine trade.","Buy-back-based mark-up operations resembling 'Inah, such as Pakistan's NIB system in the 1980s, were declared non-Shari'ah-compliant by the Shari'ah Courts."]],"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["tawarruq-vs-bai-al-inah"],"titleAlt":"Tawarruq vs Bai' al-'Inah","summary":"Section 13.3 (cross-referenced at 6.11) explains that classical Hanbali and Shafi'i jurists distinguish these two liquidity-generating sale structures mainly by who buys back the commodity, even though some later Hanafi scholars have equated the two.","source":{"chapters":[6,13],"sections":["6.11","13.3"],"pages":[148,149,349,350]},"columnsAlt":["Tawarruq","Bai' al-'Inah"]},{"id":"takaful-insurance","title":"Takaful vs conventional insurance","topicId":"t16.5","columns":["Takaful","Conventional insurance"],"rows":[["Basis","Tabarru‘ / Waqf, mutual help","Commutative sale of cover"],["Risk","Shared among participants","Transferred to insurer"],["Prohibited elements","Avoided","Riba, Gharar, Maisir"],["Surplus","Participants","Insurer"],["Premium","Adequacy","Profit maximisation"],["Investments","Shari’ah-compliant only","Includes interest-based"],["Treatment of risk","Participants relinquish ownership of their donation (Tabarru') into a common (often Waqf-structured) fund, which pays losses to any member; members mutually share the underwriting surplus or loss.","Risk is transferred to a single party, the insurance company, in exchange for premiums; the book identifies Riba, Gharar and Maisir in this structure."],["Investment of funds","The Takaful operator invests only in Shari'ah-compliant avenues and shares investment profit with participants.","Insurers invest premiums in interest-based avenues without regard to Halal or Haram."],["Pricing motive","Contribution adequacy is the main consideration, subject to the rules of equity, justice and ethics.","The risk premium is commercially driven by the desire for maximum shareholder profit."],["How losses are absorbed","First from reserves, then from interest-free (Qard al-Hasan) loans from shareholders, before any general repricing — a built-in check on overpricing.","No comparable built-in structural check; losses are managed as an ordinary commercial cost."],["Life-policy basis","Built mainly on the principles of Waqf, Tabarru' and Mudarabah; agents are paid by the company rather than out of participants' contributions.","Revolves around the element of Riba (interest, dividends, bonus paid on survival); agents are typically paid from the assured's paid premiums."],["Underwriting surplus","In the model the book considers best, the underwriting surplus/loss fully belongs to the participants and is distributed pro rata to their contributions.","Underwriting surplus is retained by the company as shareholder profit."]],"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["takaful-vs-conventional-insurance"],"titleAlt":"Takaful vs Conventional Insurance","summary":"Section 16.5 compares the two systems on objectives, structure, investment policy and returns, after Chapter 16 first explains why conventional insurance is considered non-compliant.","source":{"chapters":[16],"sections":["16.5"],"pages":[427,428]},"columnsAlt":["Takaful","Conventional Insurance"]},{"id":"sukuk-bonds-shares","title":"Sukuk vs bonds vs shares","topicId":"t15.3","columns":["Sukuk","Bonds","Shares"],"rows":[["Represents","Undivided ownership of specified assets/usufruct","Debt of issuer","Ownership of whole company"],["Return","From asset cash flows (variable or quasi-fixed)","Contractual interest","Dividends/capital gains"],["Maturity","Fixed term","Fixed term","Indefinite"],["Trading","Market price if ownership; par if debt","Market price (discounting)","Market price"],["Guarantee","No issuer guarantee of capital/profit","Issuer promises principal + interest","None"],["What the instrument represents","Certificates of equal value representing undivided ownership shares in tangible assets, usufruct, services, or a specified investment activity/project (AAOIFI definition).","A pure debt obligation of the issuer to repay principal plus a predetermined interest coupon.","—"],["Basis of the investor's return","Revenue generated by, or capital appreciation of, the underlying assets/project the certificates represent.","A fixed interest coupon, unrelated to how any underlying asset performs.","—"],["Term and asset linkage","Issued for a given period tied to specific assets or a specific project (in practice ranging from three months to about ten years).","Has a maturity date but is not tied to any particular underlying asset.","—"],["Shari'ah bases of the issue","Must be structured on a recognized Shari'ah basis — e.g. Ijarah, Salam, Istisna'a, or Shirkah (Musharakah/Mudarabah) — with real underlying assets and no Riba/Gharar.","Requires no underlying asset; can be issued purely on the strength of the issuer's general credit and an interest promise.","—"],["Comparison with company shares","Unlike shares, Sukuk represent specified assets and are issued for a given period, not indefinite ownership of a whole company.","Not directly comparable to shares; a bond is simply a debt claim regardless of the issuer's asset base.","—"]],"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["sukuk-vs-conventional-bonds"],"titleAlt":"Sukuk vs Conventional Bonds","summary":"The book grounds Sukuk in securitization of real assets, drawing a sharp line against pure interest-bearing debt instruments (15.3, 15.3.4).","source":{"chapters":[15],"sections":["15.3","15.3.4"],"pages":[391,396]},"columnsAlt":["Sukuk","Conventional Bonds"]},{"id":"wakalah-mudarabah","title":"Wakalatul Istismar vs Mudarabah","topicId":"t13.2.2","columns":["Wakalatul Istismar","Mudarabah"],"rows":[["Manager's reward","Pre-agreed fee (lump sum or % of NAV)","Agreed share of profit"],["If no profit","Fee still earned","Mudarib earns nothing"],["Loss","Investors","Rabbul-mal"],["Disclosure","Fee basis in prospectus","Profit ratio in contract"],["Basis of the manager's compensation","The Wakil earns a pre-agreed fixed fee (a lump sum, or a percentage of the investment/net asset value) irrespective of the fund's profit or loss.","The Mudarib earns a pre-agreed share of actual profit only; he gets nothing at all if the venture makes no profit or incurs a loss."],["Who bears investment loss","The investor bears the entire investment loss alone, since the agent's fee is delinked from performance.","The capital provider (Rabbul-mal) bears the financial loss on the capital; the Mudarib's only 'loss' is his uncompensated time and effort."],["Use as a Takaful operator model","In the pure Wakalah model (mainly Middle East), the operator takes an agreed percentage of participants' contributions as a fee to cover management expenses; the full underwriting surplus/loss belongs to the participants, which scholars have flagged as potentially inequitable since the operator bears no downside.","In the pure Mudarabah model (mainly Asia-Pacific), the operator and participants share investment profit and underwriting surplus on a pre-agreed ratio, but scholars object that the operator gets a share of the surplus without bearing the underwriting loss, and that Tabarru' donations cannot properly double as Mudarabah capital."],["Charging management expenses from the fund","As agent, the operator can take its management fee directly out of the fund per the pre-agreed terms.","As Mudarib, the operator cannot separately charge management expenses from the fund apart from its agreed profit share — an issue the book notes even complicates a combined Wakalah-Mudarabah Takaful model."],["Certainty of the manager's own return","The fee is certain in basis (a fixed sum or fixed percentage) even though the fund's own performance is uncertain.","The manager's own return is entirely contingent on profit — a genuinely variable entitlement, not a quasi-fixed one."],["Pre-agreement and disclosure requirement","The fee basis must be fixed and disclosed (e.g. in the fund's prospectus) before the fund is launched, and is then treated as agreed by every subscriber.","The profit-sharing ratio must be a known proportion (never a fixed amount or a percentage of capital); any ambiguity about the capital or the ratio invalidates the Mudarabah contract."]],"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["wakalah-vs-mudarabah"],"titleAlt":"Wakalah vs Mudarabah (Fund/Fee Management)","summary":"Sections 13.2.2 and 16.4 show the same fee-versus-profit-share choice recurring in both investment-fund management and Takaful operator structures, each with its own Shari'ah trade-offs.","source":{"chapters":[12,13,16],"sections":["12.4","13.2.2","16.4"],"pages":[321,349,424]},"columnsAlt":["Wakalah (Agency)","Mudarabah (Profit-Sharing)"]},{"id":"dm-milk-aqd","title":"Diminishing Musharakah: Shirkatulmilk vs Shirkatul‘aqd","topicId":"t12.8","columns":["Shirkatulmilk DM","Shirkatul‘aqd DM"],"rows":[["Purpose","Joint ownership (e.g. housing)","Profit-earning business"],["Profit ratio","Not needed; rent on bank's share","Must be agreed; may differ from equity"],["Unit price","Pre-agreed schedule allowed","Market value / price at time of sale only"],["Feasibility","Suited to leasable fixed assets","Limited — reduces equity poorly"]],"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"islamic-conventional-banking","title":"Islamic vs conventional banking","topicId":"t17.4.3","columns":["Islamic bank","Conventional bank"],"rows":[["Subject matter","Goods, assets, services","Money"],["Deposits","Qard (current), Mudarabah/Wakalah (investment)","Interest-bearing loans to bank"],["Default","Price fixed; penalty to charity","Interest accrues"],["Risk","Asset, market, Shari’ah, fiduciary risks","Mainly credit risk"],["Benchmark","Used as pricing tool for goods","Price of money"],["Money creation","Needs real assets","Credit creation"],["Subject matter of the business (extended)","Required to deal in goods, usufruct, or genuine partnership; time value of money is recognized only for pricing goods/usufruct, never for pricing money or debt itself.","Deals directly in money — lending and borrowing money against interest."],["Return after the contract is executed","Once a valid Murabaha/Ijarah receivable is created, nothing more can be added to it over time.","Interest continues to accrue on the outstanding loan/debt over time."],["Use of market benchmarks","May reference a conventional interest-based benchmark only as a pricing tool for goods/usufruct, given the small current share of Islamic finance and the lack of a mature alternative benchmark.","Uses the same interest-based benchmark, but as the price of money/debt itself."],["Fixity of return","Fixity depends on the nature of the contract: Murabaha profit can be validly fixed (as a trade price) and Ijarah rental can be validly (quasi-)fixed because the lessor bears ownership risk; a pure money/debt return can never be fixed.","Interest is fixed (or floating) purely as a charge on money/debt, without any underlying trade or ownership risk."],["Loss pass-through on deposits","Mudarabah-based deposit pools can in principle absorb loss, and in practice a large, diversified pool of financings usually earns an overall profit even if some transactions lose money.","Depositors are guaranteed their principal plus a contracted interest rate regardless of the bank's portfolio performance."]],"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["islamic-vs-conventional-banking"],"titleAlt":"Islamic Banking vs Conventional Banking","summary":"Section 17.4.3 responds directly to the common criticism that Islamic and conventional banking are practically indistinguishable, by explaining what genuinely differs even where surface practices (benchmarks, collateral) look similar.","source":{"chapters":[17],"sections":["17.4.3"],"pages":[447,448,449]},"columnsAlt":["Islamic Banking","Conventional Banking"]},{"id":"ju-alah-ijarah","title":"Ju‘alah vs Ijarah of services (Ujrah)","topicId":"t13.4.3","columns":["Ju‘alah","Ijarah / Ujrah"],"rows":[["Worker","May be unspecified (public offer)","Specified"],["Work","Result specified; effort uncertain","Work and wage specified"],["Entitlement","Only on result","For time worked"],["Acceptance","Not needed for public offer","Required"]],"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"contracts-validity","title":"Valid, voidable and void contracts","topicId":"t5.7","columns":["Sahih (valid)","Fasid (voidable — Hanafi category)","Batil (void)"],"rows":[["Essence (Asl) and attributes (Wasf)","Both correct","Essence lawful, an attribute defective","Major condition missing or illegal attribute"],["Causes","—","Coercion (Hanafi), missing information, invalid conditions","Defects in form, capacity, subject matter or delivery; Riba, Gharar, Qimar"],["Effect","Full legal effect","Ownership passes on consented possession; market value applies","No effect; goods must be returned"],["Cure","—","Remove the defect and it becomes valid","Cannot be cured"]],"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"shirkah-kinds","title":"Kinds of contractual Shirkah (Section 12.2.2)","topicId":"t12.2.2","columns":["Shirkah al ‘Inan","Mufawadah","Shirkatula‘mal","Shirkatul Wujooh","Mudarabah"],"rows":[["Subject","Joint capital","Everything equal: property, privileges, persuasion","Joint labour or crafts","Joint purchases on personal credit","Capital from one side, labour from the other"],["Status","Consensus of all jurists","Hanafi only; practically non-existent","Not recognised by Shafi‘i, Jafari and Zahiri schools, which accept only ‘Inan and Mudarabah","Shafi‘i: unlawful; Malikis: only with obligations specified; Hanafi/Hanbali: valid","Consensus of all jurists"],["Partner relation","Agent, not surety","Equal in all respects","Share earnings from work","Liability as agreed at the start","Mudarib is an agent until profit arises"],["Example","Modern business partnership","—","Doctors, teachers, transporters","Joint credit purchase for resale","Investor funds a trader"]],"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"hj-arbun","title":"Hamish Jiddiyah vs ‘Arbun (Section 5.6.1)","topicId":"t5.6.1","columns":["Hamish Jiddiyah","‘Arbun"],"rows":[["When paid","Before the sale, with a promise","At or after execution of the sale"],["Nature","Security held in trust","Part of the price"],["If buyer backs out","Only actual loss deducted; rest refunded","Seller may keep it (AAOIFI prefers refunding excess over actual loss)"],["If sale proceeds","Adjusted against the price","Counts towards the price"],["Juristic basis","Securing a promise","Hanbali view; OIC Fiqh Council and AAOIFI with a time limit"]],"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"kafalah-rahn","title":"Kafalah vs Rahn (Section 7.15)","topicId":"t7.15","columns":["Kafalah (suretyship)","Rahn / Rihn (pledge)"],"rows":[["Security given by","A third-party surety","The debtor, pledging an asset"],["What the creditor holds","A claim against the surety","The asset, in trust"],["Gains and losses","—","Belong to the pledger"],["On default","Surety pays; may receive Zakat as Gharim","Sold via court or power of attorney; excess returned; no appropriation in lieu of debt"],["Fee","Gratuitous; only expenses may be charged","No benefit to the pledgee in a loan"]],"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"qard-dayn-default","title":"Qard vs Dayn on default (Sections 7.12, 17.4.4)","topicId":"t17.4.4","columns":["Qard (loan)","Dayn from sale or Ijarah"],"rows":[["Origin","Gratuitous lending","Business transaction"],["Debtor in genuine hardship","Give more time or forgive","Give more time; no charge"],["Wilful, solvent defaulter","Give more time (penalties are approved for Dayn only)","Fine for charity plus actual loss may be claimed"],["Remission for early payment","Allowed for debts already due","Contractual rebate not allowed; bank discretion (AAOIFI)"],["Increase over principal","Riba","Riba — the price cannot be raised"]],"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"takaful-models","title":"Takaful models (Section 16.4.1)","topicId":"t16.4.1","columns":["Wakalah","Mudarabah","Wakalah–Mudarabah","Waqf / Wakalah–Waqf"],"rows":[["Operator income","Fixed % fee (e.g. 30 %)","Share of investment profit and UWS","Fee plus shares","Fee plus Mudarib share of investment profit"],["Underwriting surplus","Participants (sometimes a performance share to the operator)","Shared with operator","Shared with operator","Belongs to the Waqf fund and its beneficiaries"],["Deficit","Participants; Qard from operator","Operator bears none","Operator bears none","Reserves, then Qard from shareholders"],["Main objection","Not equitable","A donation cannot be Mudarabah capital; takes UWS not UWL","Mudarib cannot also charge fees","Recommended by 40+ scholars"]],"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"cards","title":"Debit vs charge vs credit card (Section 14.4.7)","topicId":"t14.4.7","columns":["Debit card","Charge card","Credit card"],"rows":[["Funds","Holder’s own balance","Credit up to a ceiling, paid in full on statement","Revolving credit up to a limit"],["Conventional cost","Fees for cash or FX use","Interest after the free period","Interest on the rolled-over balance"],["Islamic form","Permissible within the balance","Permissible without interest; fixed fees","Only interest-free (e.g. EIB, KFH); ‘Inah-based cards questionable"],["Permitted income","Fees; merchant commission","Membership/renewal fees; merchant commission","Fixed fees; flat cash-withdrawal fee; merchant commission"]],"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"inah-saleleaseback","title":"Bai‘ al ‘Inah vs sale and lease-back (Sections 6.11, 15.3.4)","topicId":"t15.3.4","columns":["Bai‘ al ‘Inah","Sale and lease-back"],"rows":[["Structure","Sell on credit, buy back cheaper for cash (or vice versa)","Buy an asset from a party, then lease it to that party"],["Net effect","A loan with interest","Owner bears ownership risk and earns rent"],["Juristic view","Prohibited by the jurists in general","Allowed if the sale is complete before the Ijarah"],["Safeguard","—","Repurchase at least a year later (scholars’ recommendation)"],["Use","Some Malaysian bonds and cards (criticised)","Most sovereign Ijarah Sukuk; not desirable for consumer durables"]],"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"bai-al-dayn-permitted-vs-restricted","title":"Bai' al-Dayn: Permitted vs Restricted Views","topicId":"t6.10","columns":["Permissive View (Some Malaysian Scholars/Practice)","Restrictive View (Majority / Jumhoor Ulama)"],"rows":[["Core position on discounted/premium sale of debt","Secondary-market trading of debt and debt-based securities, including Bai' al-Dayn-based Sukuk, is accepted and practised.","Traditional Muslim jurists and the overwhelming majority of contemporary Shari'ah scholars are unanimous that Bai' al-Dayn at a discount or premium is not allowed."],["Claimed juristic basis","Usually justified by reference to a ruling of the Shafi'i school.","The book notes this reliance is mistaken: Shafi'i jurists permitted selling a debt only at its par (face) value, not at a discount or premium, so the school does not actually support the permissive practice."],["Institutional consensus","Adopted in practice by some Malaysian experts and reflected in a variety of Malaysia-based Sukuk.","The OIC Islamic Fiqh Academy, whose membership includes Malaysia, has approved the prohibition of Bai' al-Dayn unanimously, without a single dissenting vote."],["Effect of asset backing on the ruling","Treats Sukuk-embedded debt as tradable in the secondary market because it is supported by real underlying assets.","The Jumhoor Ulama do not accept this reasoning even where the debt represented by Sukuk is asset-backed, since it is the debt itself, not the asset, that is being priced at a discount or premium."],["Geographic reach of the view","Confined mainly to Malaysia; the book notes the position of Malaysian Islamic bonds 'remains unacceptable among the Middle Eastern jurists.'","Represents the mainstream position across the Middle East and the broader international Shari'ah-scholarship community."]],"summary":"Section 7.16 sets out an explicit scholarly divide over trading debt-based securities such as Sukuk in the secondary market, a divide the book (6.10) grounds in the wider prohibition on selling a Dayn at other than its face value.","source":{"chapters":[6,7],"sections":["6.10","7.16"],"pages":[146,147,172]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"financial-lease-vs-operating-lease","title":"Financial Lease (Hire-Purchase) vs Operating Lease","topicId":"t11.4","columns":["Financial Lease / Hire-Purchase","Operating Lease"],"rows":[["Ownership at end of term","Structured so the lessee ends up owning the asset — at a price specified in advance or its market value at that time.","Ownership never transfers; the lessor takes back the equipment/asset when the lease ends."],["Lease duration and cost recovery","The period is long enough (usually the asset's entire useful life) for the lessor to fully amortize its cost plus a stipulated interest return.","Covers only a specified period, not the asset's whole useful life; the same asset is repeatedly re-leased to different customers as each lease ends."],["When rent liability starts","Rent liability starts from the day the lessor pays the price to the supplier, even before the lessee takes delivery of the asset.","Not described as suffering this defect in the same way — rent is for actual use of a possessed asset the lessor already owns and maintains in its own fleet."],["Who bears ownership/obsolescence risk","Risk of ownership is effectively shifted onto the lessee, especially once a residual value is fixed in advance.","The lessor retains ownership and bears the risk of obsolescence, recession or diminishing demand for the asset between leases."],["Typical use and users","Widely used by banks as a financing substitute for an outright credit purchase of equipment or assets.","Rarely used by banks; mainly used by NBFIs for specialized, high-cost, long-life assets (e.g. aircraft, ships) that require large capital outlay."],["Termination and default treatment","Not cancellable before expiry without mutual consent; the lessor can repossess without a court order on default, and prepayment or default often carries an extra fine/liquidated-damages charge (sometimes involving compound interest).","Simply ends at the agreed period, or continues only if renewed by mutual consent — there is no purchase-linked default or repossession mechanism since ownership was never intended to transfer."]],"summary":"Sections 11.4.1 and 11.4.3 describe two distinct modern leasing forms banks and NBFIs use; the book then appraises both against Shari'ah principles in 11.4.4, since neither, as conventionally practised, fully matches genuine Ijarah.","source":{"chapters":[11],"sections":["11.4.1","11.4.3","11.4.4"],"pages":[288,289]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"current-account-vs-investment-deposit","title":"Current Account vs Investment (PLS) Deposit","topicId":"t8.5.2","columns":["Current Account","Investment (PLS) Deposit"],"rows":[["Underlying contract","Structured as Qard (an interest-free loan) held as Amanah — the depositor is effectively a creditor of the bank.","Structured on a profit-and-loss-sharing (Mudarabah/Musharakah) basis — the depositor is a Rabbul-mal, and pool members are partners with each other."],["Return","No contractual return at all; some writers allow only a discretionary, non-obligatory gratis payment that must not be offered so regularly that it becomes a customary entitlement.","A share of actual profit earned by the bank's financing operations, distributed per an agreed profit-sharing ratio and weightage."],["Principal guarantee","The bank must guarantee the full principal amount of the deposit.","Principal is not guaranteed — as Rabbul-mal, the depositor bears loss in proportion to capital under Musharakah rules, absent misconduct or negligence by the bank as Mudarib."],["Weightage / pooling mechanism","No weightage system needs to be developed or applied for this type of account.","Deposits are pooled by tenor and assigned a weightage (the longer the maturity, the higher the weight) on a daily product basis, as illustrated in Box 8.1."],["Relationship type","A debtor–creditor relationship between the bank and the depositor continues throughout.","A Musharakah relationship exists among the pool's depositors, combined with a Mudarabah relationship between the pool (as Rabbul-mal) and the bank (as Mudarib)."],["Suitability","Suited to risk-averse clients who want their funds safeguarded as an interest-free loan with no exposure to the bank's financing risk.","Suited to risk-prone depositors willing to become part of the bank's earning-asset base in exchange for a variable, profit-linked return."]],"summary":"Section 8.5.2 sets out the two basic deposit products of an Islamic bank, built on entirely different contracts and risk-bearing logic — a distinction Box 8.1 then works through with an actual weightage-based profit calculation.","source":{"chapters":[8],"sections":["8.5.2"],"pages":[188,189,190,191]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"wakalah-vs-waqf-takaful-model","title":"Wakalah Model vs Waqf Model of Takaful","topicId":"t16.4.1","columns":["Pure Wakalah Model","Waqf Model"],"rows":[["Structural basis","The operator simply acts as Wakil (agent) for the participants under an agency contract.","Shareholders first create a Waqf (endowment) fund through an initial donation, to which participants' contributions are then added."],["Operator's compensation","An agreed percentage of participants' donations (e.g. around 30 %), fixed annually in consultation with the Shari'ah committee, to cover all management expenses.","A management fee (roughly 25–30 %) for investing the Waqf fund's assets on a Wakalah basis, plus a separate Mudarib profit share on the investment side."],["Ownership of underwriting surplus/loss (UWS/UWL)","The whole UWS/UWL belongs to the participants; the operator may still receive an incentive share of UWS based on performance.","UWS/UWL belongs to the Waqf fund itself, which may distribute it to beneficiaries, hold it as a reserve, or reinvest it for the Waqf's benefit — there is no obligation to distribute it."],["Who ultimately bears the underwriting loss","Borne only by the participants; the operator merely extends Qard al-Hasan if needed, which scholars flag as inequitable since the operator bears no downside.","Absorbed by the fund itself from its reserves/Waqf corpus, with shareholders providing Qard al-Hasan to the fund in case of need."],["Investment-side arrangement","The pure Wakalah model as described covers the agency relationship itself, without a distinctly separate investment contract.","A distinct Mudarabah contract governs investment of the fund's assets between the Waqf fund and the company, redeemed to the policyholder on an NAV basis at maturity."],["Scholarly assessment","Shari'ah scholars have expressed reservations about this model for not being equitable to participants.","Regarded by the Usmani-led panel of over forty scholars as the best basis (alone or combined with Wakalah) for evolving a practical, Shari'ah-compliant Takaful system."]],"summary":"Section 16.4.1 lays out several models Shari'ah scholars have proposed for structuring a Takaful operator, and reports that a panel of over forty scholars led by Shaikh Taqi Usmani judged the Waqf (or Wakalah-Waqf) model the best basis for a Shari'ah-compliant system, over the pure Wakalah model generally practised in the Middle East.","source":{"chapters":[16],"sections":["16.4.1"],"pages":[423,424,425,426]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}]});
